  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nOvernight has been comfortable, with lows in the 50s across the CWA. \nA few high level clouds remain in place, and low-lying areas could \nsee a shallow layer of fog early into the morning, especially across \nthe Bluegrass area. Tomorrow should be another lovely day, with \nhighs in the upper 70s and a light northerly breeze to keep things \ncomfortable. We sit along the leading edge of an upper-level trough \nfeeding a weak lower-level Low, which is reluctant to move in the \nnext day or two. This should leave rather clear skies tomorrow \nnight, allowing for radiational cooling down to the upper 40s and \nlower 50s. As it meanders out of the area, a weak High sets up over \nthe Great Lakes tomorrow afternoon into the evening. Expect highs in \nthe low to mid 70s as a stiffer breeze sets up light CAA, with \nsustained northeasterly speeds in the 10 knot range tomorrow \nafternoon and a few gusts around the 15 knot mark. Relative humidity \nin the 30% range will lead to a less muggy feel than we've seen in a \nfew days. Mostly clear skies and a light wind will lead to a solid \ndecoupling tomorrow night, bringing overnight lows in the lower 40s. \nA low in an isolated valley of upper 30s isn't entirely out of the \nquestion, either.\n\nTuesday - Saturday...\n\nDry conditions will continue through mid week and into late week as \nsurface high pressure controls the region beneath unremarkable/dry \nNW flow aloft. After highs pretty close to normal (low to mid 70s) \non Tuesday, temps then trend around 10 degrees above normal through \nmid to late week. Looking for low and mid 80s Wed-Sat with crisp \nmorning lows mostly in the upper 40s and low 50s.\n\nThe next chances for rain looks to arrive by late weekend as a slug \nof tropical moisture works its way northward out of the Gulf into \nthe upper ridge established over the eastern CONUS. Earlier data had \nsuggested that a trough ejecting out of the western CONUS might have \nshunted this system farther south and east, however latest data \nsuggests that western energy is much slower to eject. As a result, \ncould be set up for a round of widespread rainfall, which will \nlikely be welcome after another dry week. Right now, LREF \nprobabilities of 1\" or greater rainfall late weekend into early next \nweek is around 10% across southern IN to 25% across southern KY.",
